WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.100 --> 00:00:07.200
Caleb, a coder at the world’s largest internet
company, wins a competition to spend a week

00:00:07.200 --> 00:00:11.110
at the mountain retreat of his reclusive billionaire
boss Nathan.

00:00:11.110 --> 00:00:15.889
He cannot believe his luck and he’s even
more surprised to find he's there to interact

00:00:15.889 --> 00:00:21.310
with a female robot called Ava created by
Nathan and to find out whether she’s truly

00:00:21.310 --> 00:00:23.290
self-aware, like a human being.

00:00:23.290 --> 00:00:27.610
As I walked in to the cinema to watch Ex Machina,
my expectations were sky-high.

00:00:27.610 --> 00:00:33.340
First of all, because of the trio of uber-talented
actors who star in the film – that’s Alicia

00:00:33.340 --> 00:00:35.880
Vikander, Oscar Isaac, and Domhnall Gleeson.

00:00:35.880 --> 00:00:40.770
And second, because writer Alex Garland has
pretty much nailed every script he’s written

00:00:40.770 --> 00:00:43.159
from 28 Days Later to Dredd.

00:00:43.159 --> 00:00:48.039
Speaking of which, I would love to see a Dredd
sequel though sadly it looks like that ain’t

00:00:48.039 --> 00:00:49.039
gonna happen.

00:00:49.039 --> 00:00:53.179
Anyway, how could Ex Machina possibly live
up to my level of anticipation?

00:00:53.179 --> 00:00:56.929
Well, not only did it live up to it, but it
actually exceeded it!

00:00:56.929 --> 00:01:00.929
As well as writing Ex Machina, Alex Garland
makes his debut in the director’s chair

00:01:00.929 --> 00:01:03.690
and he excels in both departments.

00:01:03.690 --> 00:01:07.310
The script is clever and coherent while the
visuals are simply stunning.

00:01:07.310 --> 00:01:12.530
Because so much of Ex Machina is about Caleb,
Nathan and Ava getting to know each other,

00:01:12.530 --> 00:01:16.450
most of the film revolves around conversations
between them, rather than action.

00:01:16.450 --> 00:01:20.390
In the hands of Garland, this isn’t a problem
though as he’s written a dialogue-driven

00:01:20.390 --> 00:01:25.670
script that’s full of tension, deals with
big issues in a clear manner, and is consistently

00:01:25.670 --> 00:01:26.780
fascinating.

00:01:26.780 --> 00:01:31.290
In fact, I was gripped from the start right
through to the end by Garland’s perfect

00:01:31.290 --> 00:01:35.970
blend of intelligent sci-fi and psychological
thriller infused with horror.

00:01:35.970 --> 00:01:40.090
And he’s also managed to inject a good dose
of humour into the film too, sometimes in

00:01:40.090 --> 00:01:43.710
the most unlikely moments, and this works
just wonderfully.

00:01:43.710 --> 00:01:48.159
Look out for one of the best dance scenes
I’ve seen in a very long time!

00:01:48.159 --> 00:01:49.159
Not only has Garland done a top job in the
writer’s and director’s chair, he’s

00:01:49.159 --> 00:01:53.450
also assembled a crack team of on-screen and
behind-the-scenes talent who really make the

00:01:53.450 --> 00:01:54.450
film pop.

00:01:54.450 --> 00:01:58.210
If you’ve watched my review of the wartime
drama Testament Of Youth, you’ll know how

00:01:58.210 --> 00:02:00.030
highly I think of Swedish actress Alicia Vikander.

00:02:00.030 --> 00:02:03.490
You can click here to watch that video.

00:02:03.490 --> 00:02:07.899
And she’s just as dazzling again in Ex Machina,
giving another finely honed performance as

00:02:07.899 --> 00:02:09.190
female robot Ava.

00:02:09.190 --> 00:02:14.830
Vikander’s speech, facial expressions, gestures
and gait all combine beautifully to suggest

00:02:14.830 --> 00:02:16.770
an otherness intermingled with humanity.

00:02:16.770 --> 00:02:21.400
And you can sense Vikander’s early training
as a ballerina in the way she’s able to

00:02:21.400 --> 00:02:26.940
completely control her body, brilliantly blurring
the line between how humans and robots move.

00:02:26.940 --> 00:02:33.020
Oscar Isaac brings Nathan to life oh-so superbly,
which is hardly surprising given his past

00:02:33.020 --> 00:02:37.310
performances in films like Drive, Inside Llewyn
Davis, and A Most Violent Year.

00:02:37.310 --> 00:02:42.300
He’s one of those actors who can totally
vanish into a role, and he does that to great

00:02:42.300 --> 00:02:43.910
effect in Ex Machina.

00:02:43.910 --> 00:02:47.810
Isaac cleverly balances his performance on
a knife-edge between what Nathan’s like

00:02:47.810 --> 00:02:50.420
on the surface and what he’s like in reality.

00:02:50.420 --> 00:02:55.540
So, despite Nathan’s super-chill demeanour
and his apparent air of down-to-earth matey-ness,

00:02:55.540 --> 00:02:59.750
there’s more than a trace of the supreme
control freak about him.

00:02:59.750 --> 00:03:04.670
Between his superb turn in Ex Machina, and
roles coming up in Star Wars and X-Men: Apocalypse,

00:03:04.670 --> 00:03:08.360
the next 18 months are going to be huge for
Isaac!

00:03:08.360 --> 00:03:13.160
Just like Isaac and Vikander, Domhnall Gleeson’s
star is very much on the rise this year; with

00:03:13.160 --> 00:03:16.590
Episode 7 among a number of films you’ll
see him in in 2015.

00:03:16.590 --> 00:03:21.530
Gleeson’s worked before with both Vikander
and Alex Garland on Joe Wright’s adaptation

00:03:21.530 --> 00:03:25.050
of Anna Karenina and on the sci-fi action
movie Dredd.

00:03:25.050 --> 00:03:29.400
And he impresses yet again in Ex Machina with
an intelligent and natural performance as

00:03:29.400 --> 00:03:30.690
the softly-spoken Caleb.

00:03:30.690 --> 00:03:34.900
Oh, and if you want to find out more about
what the year ahead holds for Oscar Isaac,

00:03:34.900 --> 00:03:40.290
Domhnall Gleeson and Alicia Vikander, click
here to watch my video on the 10 Actors You

00:03:40.290 --> 00:03:42.100
Must Watch in 2015!

00:03:42.100 --> 00:03:46.980
Much-deserved props also go to cinematographer
Rob Hardy, who’s never worked on a sci-fi

00:03:46.980 --> 00:03:47.980
movie before.

00:03:47.980 --> 00:03:53.310
Hardy did an exceptional job on Testament
Of Youth and his work on Ex Machina is outstanding.

00:03:53.310 --> 00:03:57.340
Comic book artist Jock, who did the early
concept design for Ava, and the movie’s

00:03:57.340 --> 00:04:02.220
visual effects team have also done an absolutely
stellar job bringing Ava to life.

00:04:02.220 --> 00:04:07.410
And I must also mention production designer
Mark Digby and set decorator Michelle Day

00:04:07.410 --> 00:04:11.660
whose work is so crucial to the whole eerily
beautiful feel of the film.

00:04:11.660 --> 00:04:17.280
Although just outside Nathan’s home-come-research-facility
there are acres of gorgeous wilderness filled

00:04:17.280 --> 00:04:22.000
with mountains, trees and waterfalls, the
building’s geographical isolation coupled

00:04:22.000 --> 00:04:27.270
with the fact that Caleb’s more or less
confined there gives the whole film a decidedly

00:04:27.270 --> 00:04:28.350
claustrophobic atmosphere.

00:04:28.350 --> 00:04:33.870
Add to that the building’s sleek lines and
toned-down modernist feel, and the whole space

00:04:33.870 --> 00:04:36.270
feels strangely unsettling.

00:04:36.270 --> 00:04:42.510
To sum up: Ex Machina is a superlative sci-fi
flick that benefits from an outstanding script,

00:04:42.510 --> 00:04:45.630
pitch-perfect performances and stunning visuals.

00:04:45.630 --> 00:04:47.280
What’s not to like?!

00:04:47.280 --> 00:04:50.500
Ex Machina gets 5 out of 5 Flicks.

